ace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$"] [17592186134133 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{3}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1).$"] [17592186134134 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$"] [17592186134135 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186134136 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$"] [17592186134137 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186134138 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$"] [17592186134139 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=2xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186134140 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186134212 "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$"] [17592186134213 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$"] [17592186134276 "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized \n by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}-t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]?$"] [17592186134277 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = 3x$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}}{3},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t⟩\\hspace{0.167em}\n$$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$."] [17592186134338 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}t+t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3(\\operatorname{sin}t-t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $0 \\le t \\le 2π$."] [17592186134367 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise."] [17592186134368 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=2x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186134369 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}\\pi z+2xz)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,0).$"] [17592186134370 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$, what is $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$?"] [17592186134371 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\operatorname{cos}yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\operatorname{ln}xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1, \\frac{π}{2}, 1)$."] [17592186134375 "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,3)$ and $(-1,3),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$  where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3xy,{x}^{2}y⟩,$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186134376 "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [17592186134377 "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation."] [17592186134378 "![](images/6f/6f51a25843259881994e3dca0781b780878bec349930b76fbf449dc824e69ec2.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186134386 "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)=2\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region."] [17592186134511 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em},$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6}].$"] [17592186134512 "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4yz\\cos xds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$."] [17592186134513 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{3} - y)ds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}$ for $-1 \\le t \\le 2$?"] [17592186134566 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),t+\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)⟩,\\hspace{1em}0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $\\mathbf{F}=x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative.*"] [17592186134567 "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186161896 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k\n$$"] [17592186162326 "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2\n$$ for some function $f(x, y)$, where $C_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le t \\le 1$.\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds =-2\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)ds = 4,\n$$ where $C_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(u)=⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}4{u}^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le u \\le \\frac{1}{2}$."] [17592186167050 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186167090 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2ay\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186172280 "Determine the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186172281 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186172291 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (1,1)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y\\ne 0\\}$"]}
